<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PROPOSAL 46 M1/M2 — the window layout is written by ONE method, and a
  headless run never writes it.

  THE BUG THIS BRANCH FIXES IS NOT WHAT THIS CASE GATES, and that is worth
  being blunt about. The defect was that `SSettings::setWindowState()` was
  called from exactly one place — `SMainWindow::closeEvent` — and the app's
  own Quit never reached it: File -> Exit ran `::exit(0)`, and on macOS Qt
  gives an action titled "Exit" the QuitRole, so Cmd-Q IS that slot. Measured
  on the reporting machine: no `ui/windowState` key in smaragd.ini at all, and
  ZERO occurrences of the post-exec() "Smaragd exiting" line in a 4.8 MB log.

  That WIRING has no headless route. `fileExit()` now goes through `close()`,
  whose `promptSaveUnsavedChanges()` is a modal exec() and whose success ends
  the process; `aboutToQuit` is the same. Both are hand-verified and the PR
  says so.

  What IS gated here, and both halves are real properties:

    1. `saveWindowLayout()` produces a non-empty blob under BOTH keys. The old
       code took `saveGeometry()`/`saveState()` inline at the one call site, so
       nothing anywhere ever checked that the pair it stores is usable.

    2. THE "test-case" SUPPRESSION. Every automatic caller (closeEvent,
       aboutToQuit, the M2 autosave timer) leaves `force` false, and an
       unforced call in a headless run must write NOTHING. `ctest -j4` is four
       processes sharing one smaragd.ini, and a headless run's geometry
       describes a window that is never shown at all — restoring it into a
       real session would place the docks from a window nobody ever mapped.

  THIS CASE OWNS NO SETTINGS KEY and needs no key-ownership declaration,
  unlike every `set-option` case: the verb snapshots `ui/windowGeometry` and
  `ui/windowState`, restores them (removing them again when they were absent)
  BEFORE it asserts anything, and so leaves the INI byte-for-byte as it found
  it whether it passes or fails.

  Watched failing both ways on a sabotaged binary: with the isTestCaseMode()
  guard removed the `expectWritten="false"` line fails; with saveWindowLayout()
  made an unconditional `return false` the `expectWritten="true"` line fails.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="window_layout_save">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- (2) The suppression: the call every automatic caller makes writes
         nothing at all in a "test-case" run. -->
    <save-window-layout force="false" expectWritten="false"/>

    <!-- (1) Forced, the method does its job: both keys, both non-empty. -->
    <save-window-layout force="true" expectWritten="true"/>

    <!-- ...and it is REPEATABLE, which the M2 autosave timer depends on. A
         one-shot latch would pass the line above and fail this one. -->
    <save-window-layout force="true" expectWritten="true"/>

    <!-- The suppression again, AFTER a forced write: the guard is a property
         of the call, not a first-call-only condition. -->
    <save-window-layout force="false" expectWritten="false"/>
  </actions>

</SActionScript>
